TI  - Efficient Parquet Parsing on FPGAs
PY  - 2026
T2  - 22nd International Workshop on Data Management on New Hardware, DaMoN 2026
C7  - 3
DO  - 10.1145/3789237.3809124
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045467409?origin=resultslist
AB  - Modern analytical data systems on disaggregated architectures, like Lakehouses, offer flexibility for managing semi-structured data, yet they face a critical bottleneck: the repeated deserialization of large amounts of data in complex formats like Apache Parquet require efficient execution. One option is processing closer to the data with suitable, specialized hardware, which allows for tailoring its architecture to the repeated operations. Out of the wide variety of programmable hardware in the data path, in this paper, we investigate whether Field programmable Gate Array (FPGA)-based Parquet-to-Arrow deserializers could compete with the already well optimized and parallelized software baselines in efficiency. Guided by a study of real-world Parquet files from SAP’s Lakehouse, our design implements the most relevant feature subset and supports dynamic reconfiguration of encoding and data types without requiring FPGA reprogramming. We evaluate our FPGA solution and show that it is almost 3.5× more energy efficient than a server CPU parsing the same dataset with 32 threads while showing comparable performance. These results highlight the potential of heterogeneous systems to alleviate deserialization overheads in Lakehouse systems and motivate further hardware/software co-design for data-intensive workloads. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
